Keep per-URL state of the **earliest** and **latest** versions seen (by `FetchTime`). Each message updates `first` if its FetchTime is lower, `last` if higher; the output combines them. Duplicates and stale messages change nothing → return `nil` (no downstream write), which also makes the processor **idempotent** under at-least-once delivery.

```go
type Document struct {
	URL            string
	PubDate        uint64
	FetchTime      uint64
	Text           string
	FirstFetchTime *uint64
}

type state struct {
	first Document
	last  Document
}

type Store interface {
	Get(url string) (state, bool, error)
	Put(url string, s state) error
}

type Processor struct {
	mu    sync.Mutex
	store Store
}

func (p *Processor) Process(doc *Document) (*Document, error) {
	p.mu.Lock()
	defer p.mu.Unlock()

	st, ok, err := p.store.Get(doc.URL)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	if !ok {
		st = state{first: *doc, last: *doc}
	} else {
		changed := false
		if doc.FetchTime < st.first.FetchTime {
			st.first, changed = *doc, true
		}
		if doc.FetchTime > st.last.FetchTime {
			st.last, changed = *doc, true
		}
		if !changed {
			return nil, nil
		}
	}
	if err := p.store.Put(doc.URL, st); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	first := st.first.FetchTime
	return &Document{
		URL:            doc.URL,
		PubDate:        st.first.PubDate,
		FetchTime:      st.last.FetchTime,
		Text:           st.last.Text,
		FirstFetchTime: &first,
	}, nil
}
```

Run on inputs with FetchTime 2, 1, 3, 3 (duplicate) the outputs were `PubDate/FetchTime/Text/First` = `20 2 v2 2`, `10 2 v2 1`, `10 3 v3 1`, then `nil`.

Scaling discussion: only store what's needed (first's PubDate+FetchTime, last's Text+FetchTime). Partition the input topic by `Url` so one consumer owns a URL — then the global mutex becomes per-partition and no cross-instance locking is needed. The state store must be durable (Redis/Cassandra/RocksDB state store) and updated with CAS or per-key serialization; commit input offsets only after the state write and output publish (at-least-once is fine because processing is idempotent). Old URL state can be TTL-evicted.

Each topic is a FIFO buffer guarded by a mutex; `Receive` blocks until a message arrives. The wake-up uses a "broadcast channel": `Send` closes the topic's current `ready` channel and replaces it, so all waiters wake and re-check under the lock (a closed channel never loses a wake-up, unlike `sync.Cond` it composes with `ctx.Done()` in a `select`). Popping under the lock guarantees each message goes to exactly one receiver.

```go
type topic struct {
	msgs  [][]byte
	ready chan struct{}
}

type Broker struct {
	mu     sync.Mutex
	topics map[string]*topic
}

func NewBroker() *Broker { return &Broker{topics: make(map[string]*topic)} }

func (b *Broker) topic(name string) *topic {
	t, ok := b.topics[name]
	if !ok {
		t = &topic{ready: make(chan struct{})}
		b.topics[name] = t
	}
	return t
}

func (b *Broker) Send(name string, msg []byte) {
	b.mu.Lock()
	defer b.mu.Unlock()
	t := b.topic(name)
	t.msgs = append(t.msgs, msg)
	close(t.ready)
	t.ready = make(chan struct{})
}

func (b *Broker) Receive(ctx context.Context, name string) ([]byte, error) {
	for {
		b.mu.Lock()
		t := b.topic(name)
		if len(t.msgs) > 0 {
			msg := t.msgs[0]
			t.msgs[0] = nil
			t.msgs = t.msgs[1:]
			b.mu.Unlock()
			return msg, nil
		}
		ready := t.ready
		b.mu.Unlock()

		select {
		case <-ready:
		case <-ctx.Done():
			return nil, ctx.Err()
		}
	}
}
```

Verified with 3 writers × 100 messages and 4 readers under `-race`: 300 unique messages, 0 duplicates.

Discussion points: a per-topic lock (or `sync.Map` of topics) reduces contention; a buffered channel per topic is simpler but has a fixed capacity (senders block when full, violating "send without readers"); thundering-herd wake-ups can be avoided with a per-waiter channel queue; unbounded memory needs a limit/backpressure; "exactly once" here means no duplicate hand-off — there's no ack/redelivery if a reader crashes after receiving.
